WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.309 --> 00:00:05.540
If you manage to snag a huge celebrity for
your project, most filmmakers would take advantage

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:06.950
and give them a huge role.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:09.100
But, that's not always the case.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:14.130
Sometimes, people are perfectly happy paying
top-dollar to use an ultra-star for two lines,

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.420
or two seconds of screen time.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:20.449
As the old saying goes, there are no small
parts, only small actors.

00:00:20.449 --> 00:00:24.890
Then again, some of these examples stretch
that expression to its breaking point.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:28.189
Here are some major actors hired to do very
little.

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:30.820
Vin Diesel: Guardians of the Galaxy

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:36.600
Marvel paid the Fast and Furious star Vin
Diesel a whole lot to say just three words:

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.100
"I am Groot."

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.660
Over and over again.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.210
"I am Groot."

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.640
"Yea, you said that."

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.390
Yes, he says it different each time depending
on the situation,

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.850
"I am Groot."

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:55.680
but he's still technically repeating three
words for an entire movie.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.490
And yet, each word is more memorable than
anything he said in The Pacifier.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:03.190
"Do the Peter Panda dance"

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:06.240
"Just hop three times like a kangaroo."

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.479
Now, let's all go back to forgetting that
movie exists.

00:01:09.479 --> 00:01:14.780
But, despite the simplicity of playing Groot,
Diesel is actually saying something different

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:15.780
each time.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.399
He has a special script that gives him the
English translation of whatever Groot is saying

00:01:20.399 --> 00:01:23.090
so he can more easily get into the tree's
head.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.179
"The Gunn/Diesel version of the script."

00:01:25.179 --> 00:01:26.179
"To be fair."

00:01:26.179 --> 00:01:29.580
"Where Groot's dialogue is not, 'I am Groot.'"

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:30.580
See?

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.649
It's not just Rocket who can understand him.

00:01:32.649 --> 00:01:37.189
Regardless, of the work Diesel is or isn't
doing with the role, it's still impressive

00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:40.840
that the actor at least attempts to do the
Baby Groot voice naturally.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.640
Next stop: voicing all the chipmunks in the
next, inevitable Alvin sequel.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.060
(humming Funky Town)

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.679
Or not.

00:01:49.679 --> 00:01:52.509
Jay Leno and George Clooney: South Park

00:01:52.509 --> 00:01:57.789
These days, if South Park snags a celebrity
cameo, they'll likely milk it like any other

00:01:57.789 --> 00:01:58.789
show would.

00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:03.869
But, that wasn't the case early on, and the
hit animated series used to treat celebrities

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:07.920
exactly how you'd expect them to: like they
barely exist.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.090
In one of the show's first episodes, creators
Trey Parker and Matt Stone somehow snagged

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.140
Jay Leno.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.760
And they had him play a cat.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.540
Not, like, a magical talking cat.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.629
Just a normal house cat, doing mostly normal
house cat things.

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:26.920
They hired the biggest talk show host on the
planet to meow a few times and go home.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:28.420
"Meow."

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.360
"No kitty, this is my corned beef cabbage."

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.100
"Meow."

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.300
"No kitty that's a bad kitty!"

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.040
"hsss!"

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:39.660
Amazingly, only a couple episodes earlier,
Parker and Stone cast George Clooney in "Big

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.890
Gay Al's Big Gay Boat Ride."

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:46.540
Clooney played Stan's gay dog, who woofed
like, well, pretty much any other dog.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:47.540
"Sparky!

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:48.939
Where'd you get that pink scarf?"

00:02:48.939 --> 00:02:49.939
"Woof woof!"

00:02:49.939 --> 00:02:54.239
They land two of the biggest stars in show
business, and the South Park guys hired them

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:56.080
to sound like house pets.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.040
John Hurt: Spaceballs

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.650
Sometimes when you parody something, the thing
you're poking fun at gets in on the act.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:08.370
That was the case in 1987, when actor John
Hurt showed up in Mel Brooks' Spaceballs for

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:13.360
about a minute...to do the exact same thing
he did in Alien:

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.220
"Oh no! not again!"

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.840
Of course, where Hurt's original Alien role
was the stuff of straight-up nightmares, his

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:30.780
time with Spaceballs was a bit more fun — and
somewhat less likely to haunt your dreams

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.140
until the end of time.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:36.300
The best part about this brief scene is how
the joke works for everybody.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:41.459
Even if you never saw Alien, you can appreciate
this - thing - popping out of a guy's stomach

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:44.140
and doing its best "Michigan J. Frog" impression..

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:48.760
"Hello my baby hello my honey hello my ragtime
gal.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:53.160
Send me a kiss by wire, baby my heart's on
fire!"

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:58.540
"If you refuse me honey don't lose me, then
you'll be left alone oh baby telephone, and

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:02.080
tell me I'm your own!"

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:04.620
Cate Blanchett: Hot Fuzz

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:08.079
Actor Cate Blanchett's a pretty big deal,
in case you missed it.

00:04:08.079 --> 00:04:12.440
So it makes perfect sense that she'd be in
a big-deal movie like Hot Fuzz, right?

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:16.739
What makes less sense, however, is how she
shows up for less than two minutes, never

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:22.360
shows her face, never appears again, and never
gets credited for her role, despite that role

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.210
being that of the main character's estranged
girlfriend.

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:26.210
"Hello?

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:27.240
"Janine, it's me."

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:28.250
"I know.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:29.770
I'm at work."

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:30.789
"I know.

00:04:30.789 --> 00:04:32.840
I'm outside."

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.880
Blanchett's Janine is a crime scene investigator
clad head-to-toe in a white examiner suit,

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:43.430
which leads to the wonderful scene where Simon
Pegg starts talking to her, only to realize

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:45.980
he's talking to the wrong person in a white
suit.

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:49.140
"Janine, I've been transferred, I'm moving
away for a while."

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:50.900
"I'm not Janine."

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.720
"Janine, I've been transferred, I'm moving
away for a while."

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:58.220
He and the real Janine have an argument about
Pegg's character being a workaholic.

00:04:58.220 --> 00:04:59.720
And that's it.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.090
That's all Cate Blanchett did in this film.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:06.810
But since the scene was so delightful, and
delightfully British, it's also all she needed

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:07.810
to do.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:09.949
Kurt Russell: Forrest Gump

00:05:09.949 --> 00:05:14.830
One of the first famous people young Forrest
Gump mingled with was a pre-fame Elvis Presley.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:19.669
At the time, he was just some southern kid
with a guitar — though Forrest did accidentally

00:05:19.669 --> 00:05:22.370
teach The King his signature dance move.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:28.250
"I started moving around to the music, swinging
my hips."

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:33.060
The guy physically playing Elvis is just Some
Guy, but the voice is none other than Kurt

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:37.320
Russell, who showed up to sing a little bit
of "Hound Dog" in the voiceover booth:

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.980
"You ain't nothing but a…"

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:42.160
"I liked that guitar."

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:43.240
"hound dog.."

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:44.240
"It sounded good."

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:46.010
crying all the time"

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:49.700
Voicing Elvis was, in a way, taking it full
circle for Russell.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:54.220
Fifteen years before Forrest Gump, he had
played Elvis in a movie called, appropriately

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.120
enough, Elvis: The Movie.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:00.990
But he didn't actually get to sing — country
star Ronnie McDowell handled that.

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:05.770
But thanks to Forrest, Russell can finally
say he played the Elvis role completely.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:07.870
It just took him a while to get there.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:11.509
Brad Pitt &amp; Matt Damon: Confessions Of A Dangerous
Mind

00:06:11.509 --> 00:06:16.819
Based on the not-true story of Dating Game
creator Chuck Barris being a CIA assassin,

00:06:16.819 --> 00:06:21.410
Confessions of a Dangerous Mind sported an
all star director in George Clooney.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:26.590
He naturally used his star power to bring
in fellow superstars, such as Matt Damon and

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:31.370
Brad Pitt, and naturally had them do … well,
pretty much nothing.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:35.599
Literally the only time you see either Damon
or Pitt is when they appear on Barris' Dating

00:06:35.599 --> 00:06:36.599
Game.

00:06:36.599 --> 00:06:40.229
They never say anything, and they never even
attempt to woo the girl.

00:06:40.229 --> 00:06:43.280
They just sit there as the camera quickly
pans past them.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:44.280
That's all.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.699
You barely have enough time to process the
fact that two of the biggest stars on the

00:06:47.699 --> 00:06:50.669
planet are playing the equivalent of movie
extras.

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:56.610
Finally, the camera focuses on Bachelor #3,
a schlubby guy who inexplicably wins anyway,

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.770
beating out his two gorgeous contestants.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:02.879
Still, the audience getting to see two of
the top actors in the world for a whopping

00:07:02.879 --> 00:07:08.199
ten seconds only to never see them again is
a bigger punchline than all of Barris' insane

00:07:08.199 --> 00:07:09.800
stories combined.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:11.830
Glenn Close: Hook

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:12.830
Don't feel bad.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:16.229
There's a good chance you didn't notice Glenn
Close in Hook at all.

00:07:16.229 --> 00:07:20.190
But there she is, playing one of Captain Hook's
many underling pirates.

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:24.389
But no matter how much you pause and stare
at the screen to catch a tiny glimpse of Close

00:07:24.389 --> 00:07:26.740
in the crowd, you won't find her.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:28.710
That's because she's not in the crowd.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:33.130
Instead, she's the bearded pirate getting
brutally punished for betting against Hook's

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:34.130
success.

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:37.009
It's amazing what happens when you put down
the razor for a few days.

00:07:37.009 --> 00:07:42.789
Yes, that's Close, playing a bearded, male
pirate named Gutless, sporting manlier whiskers

00:07:42.789 --> 00:07:45.669
than pretty much any guy could ever hope to
grow.

00:07:45.669 --> 00:07:50.139
Living up to his name, Gutless immediately
starts groveling and crying when he realizes

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:51.940
that Hook knows he's a traitor.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:54.360
"Say it.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:56.180
Say it."

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:58.080
"I did."

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.060
"Yes you did a boo boo."

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:00.340
"I did."

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:05.419
He's then tossed into the "Boo Box," a treasure
chest that the other pirates throw live scorpions

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:11.289
into, until Gutless apparently dies from being,
umm, scorpioned to death.

00:08:11.289 --> 00:08:14.240
Dan Aykroyd: Indiana Jones and the Temple
of Doom

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:17.460
Dan Aykroyd has one of the more distinct voices
in Hollywood, so it's a shocker that he was

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:28.420
actually able to pull off an uncredited cameo
and have so few people realize it was him.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:32.349
And yet, he did just that with Indiana Jones
and the Temple Of Doom.

00:08:32.349 --> 00:08:37.031
He's the guy escorting Indy and his gal pal
- along with Short Round - onto a plane, all

00:08:37.031 --> 00:08:40.229
while apologizing that they'll have to ride
with a bunch of chickens.

00:08:40.229 --> 00:08:45.950
In the 15 or so seconds you see Aykroyd on-screen,
you never actually see his face close up,

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:49.560
which explains a lot of why his cameo went
so under the radar for viewers.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:51.220
"I'm Art Weber.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:54.720
I spoke with your assistant, I managed to
secure three seats."

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:57.100
Michael Bay: Mystery Men

00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:02.300
While he's not an actor per se, Michael Bay
is as famous as anyone he directs.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:07.089
And whenever he insists his movies are actually
good, we're forced to suspend disbelief more

00:09:07.089 --> 00:09:10.190
than we do whenever Tom Hanks plays a mean
guy.

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:15.420
Seriously, we're just not buying it, Tom.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:20.170
But Bay managed to get himself in front of
the camera a while back, albeit briefly.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:25.610
If you watched Ben Stiller's 1999 superhero
spoof Mystery Men...and judging by the box

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:30.350
office take, not many of you did...you would
have seen Michael Bay for a glorious four

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:31.350
seconds.

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:34.370
He's the fratty doofus bro at the evil guy's
meeting.

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:35.850
And that's basically it.

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:39.650
He doesn't even come back to hit Ben Stiller
in the head with an evil beer can.

00:09:39.650 --> 00:09:44.330
Really, if you think about it, it's absolutely
perfect that Bay played a frat guy — that

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:47.760
is, after all, his target demographic.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.460
Thanks for watching!

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:00.380
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.820
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love too!

